17592186069606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069605 #edb/ref 1061 145542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "461b27d5-3e08-5e71-9e57-464e06a7b559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069606 #edb/ref 1061 145546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95df2774-fa6c-5c44-85c3-1203c060ec1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069607 #edb/ref 1061 145550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3706f0b6-4695-54b2-974b-2b4f77638739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069608 #edb/ref 1061 145551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "419f8ba9-27c0-5918-8ecd-eff659df7bb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070026 #edb/ref 1064 "**Absolute value inequalities** combine two basic concepts: absolute value and inequality. The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero, while an inequality specifies a range of values.\n\nConsider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| < 7\n$$\n\nThis inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7 < x < 7\n$$\n\nTo solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n|2x+3| \\leq 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5.$ So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq 2x+3 \\leq 5\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\le 2x+3\\le 5 \\\\ -8\\le 2x\\le 2 \\\\ -4\\le x\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070026 #edb/ref 1065 8825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070026 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "53ecd5cc-486a-584d-abc8-f75ec26f12cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070026 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070027 #edb/ref 1064 "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the \"greater than\" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:\n\n$$\n|x| \\geq 6\n$$\n\nTo solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -6 & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the word \"or\" instead of \"and\" since only one of the inequalities $x\\leq-6$ or $x\\geq 6$ needs to be true for $|x| \\geq 6$ to be true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070027 #edb/ref 1065 8830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070027 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "93e8452a-53ea-5e58-807a-00e95af99af1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070027 #edb/ref 1067 "Greater Than (or Equal To) Absolute Value Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
